\title{Reticulations, Cooperative Systems, and Rectangular Latin Structures}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite coordinate theory unifying two-type incidence structures, rectangular Latin conditions, orthogonal matrix pairs, and balanced arrays. A reticulation consists of a finite point set equipped with two classes of coordinate families such that every coordinate map chosen from opposite classes jointly labels the points bijectively. A cooperative system consists of column-Latin matrices and row-Latin matrices, every cross-type pair being orthogonal. A svelte semi-orthogonal array records the same cross-coordinate condition row by row. We prove unique-position and unique-intersection properties, characterize column- and row-Latin matrices by orthogonality to canonical coordinate matrices, and give conversions among the three presentations. The central counting consequence is that every nonempty two-sided finite reticulation has exactly $mn$ points, and every corresponding svelte array has exactly $mn$ rows. We also describe validation and conversion algorithms and applications to balanced design and cross-view indexing.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Classical nets, orthogonal arrays, and mutually orthogonal Latin squares encode a common phenomenon: independently meaningful partitions of a finite set interact with exact regularity. The square case can obscure two useful freedoms. The two sides may have different alphabet sizes $m$ and $n$, and compatibility may be required only across two types of families rather than between every pair.

There are three natural presentations. In the incidence presentation, points lie on fibres called lines, with weft and warp lines intersecting uniquely. In the matrix presentation, an $m$-symbol matrix is Latin down columns, an $n$-symbol matrix is Latin across rows, and opposite-type matrices are orthogonal. In the array presentation, rows carry several left and right coordinates, and every projection onto one left and one right coordinate is a complete rectangular grid.

The basic hypothesis is bijectivity. For a weft coordinate $w_u:P\to[m]$ and a warp coordinate $z_v:P\to[n]$, require
\[
p\longmapsto (w_u(p),z_v(p))
\]
to be a bijection for every $u$ and $v$. Here $[k]=\{0,1,\ldots,k-1\}$. This condition yields unique intersections, a grid representation, and the cardinality $|P|=mn$.

\section{Definitions}
For positive integers $m,n$, put $\Grid=\I m\times\I n$. An $a$-symbol matrix of shape $m\times n$ is a function $M:\Grid\to\I a$.

\begin{definition}[Column-Latin matrix]
A matrix $C:\Grid\to\I m$ is \emph{column-Latin} if, for every $j\in\I n$, the map $i\mapsto C(i,j)$ is a bijection $\I m\to\I m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Row-Latin matrix]
A matrix $R:\Grid\to\I n$ is \emph{row-Latin} if, for every $i\in\I m$, the map $j\mapsto R(i,j)$ is a bijection $\I n\to\I n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Orthogonality]
Matrices $C:\Grid\to\I m$ and $R:\Grid\to\I n$ are \emph{orthogonal} if
\[
\Phi_{C,R}:\Grid\to\I m\times\I n,\qquad p\mapsto(C(p),R(p))
\]
is bijective. Thus every ordered pair of symbols occurs at exactly one position.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cooperative systems]
A \emph{cooperative pair} is a pair $(C,R)$ in which $C$ is column-Latin, $R$ is row-Latin, and they are orthogonal. Given index sets $U,V$, a \emph{cooperative system} consists of column-Latin matrices $C_u$ for $u\in U$ and row-Latin matrices $R_v$ for $v\in V$, such that $C_u$ and $R_v$ are orthogonal for every $(u,v)\in U\times V$. No same-type orthogonality is required.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coordinate reticulation]
Let $P$ be finite. A \emph{coordinate reticulation} with parameters $(m,n)$ consists of maps
\[
w_u:P\to\I m\quad(u\in U),\qquad z_v:P\to\I n\quad(v\in V)
\]
such that $p\mapsto(w_u(p),z_v(p))$ is bijective for every $u,v$. The fibres $w_u^{-1}(q)$ are weft lines and $z_v^{-1}(r)$ are warp lines. For each family, the fibres partition $P$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Svelte semi-orthogonal array]
A \emph{svelte semi-orthogonal array} has a finite row set $Y$ and entries
\[
L:Y\times U\to\I m,\qquad T:Y\times V\to\I n,
\]
such that $y\mapsto(L(y,u),T(y,v))$ is a bijection from $Y$ to $\I m\times\I n$ for every $u,v$. Thus each cross-type ordered pair occurs exactly once.
\end{definition}

\section{Local regularity and intersections}
\begin{theorem}[Unique column position]
If $C$ is column-Latin, then for every $j\in\I n$ and $q\in\I m$ there is a unique $i\in\I m$ such that $C(i,j)=q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For fixed $j$, the map $i\mapsto C(i,j)$ is bijective. Surjectivity gives a preimage of $q$, and injectivity makes it unique.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique row position]
If $R$ is row-Latin, then for every $i\in\I m$ and $r\in\I n$ there is a unique $j\in\I n$ such that $R(i,j)=r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply surjectivity and injectivity of the fixed-row permutation $j\mapsto R(i,j)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique cross-intersection]
If $(C,R)$ is a cooperative pair, then for every $(q,r)\in\I m\times\I n$ there is a unique $p\in\Grid$ satisfying $C(p)=q$ and $R(p)=r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The ordered pair $(q,r)$ has exactly one preimage under the bijection $p\mapsto(C(p),R(p))$.
\end{proof}

Hence the fibres $C^{-1}(q)$ and $R^{-1}(r)$ meet at exactly one point.

\section{Canonical coordinate matrices}
Define
\[
H(i,j)=i,\qquad V(i,j)=j.
\]
The pair $(H,V)$ is cooperative because their combined map is the identity on $\Grid$.

\begin{theorem}[Column-coordinate characterization]
A matrix $C:\Grid\to\I m$ is column-Latin if and only if it is orthogonal to $V$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $C$ is column-Latin, then for any $(q,j)$ there is a unique $i$ with $C(i,j)=q$; thus $(i,j)$ is the unique preimage of $(q,j)$ under $p\mapsto(C(p),V(p))$.

Conversely, suppose this global map is bijective. If $C(i,j)=C(i',j)$, then $(C(i,j),j)=(C(i',j),j)$, so injectivity gives $i=i'$. Given $(q,j)$, surjectivity gives a point whose second coordinate is necessarily $j$ and whose $C$-value is $q$. Thus each column map is bijective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Row-coordinate characterization]
A matrix $R:\Grid\to\I n$ is row-Latin if and only if $H$ is orthogonal to $R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the row--column dual of the preceding proof. The global pair $(i,R(i,j))$ occurs exactly once precisely when every symbol occurs exactly once in each fixed row.
\end{proof}

These results replace a collection of local permutation conditions by a single global orthogonality test.

\section{Conversions among presentations}
Let $((C_u)_{u\in U},(R_v)_{v\in V})$ be a cooperative system on $\Grid$.

\begin{theorem}[Induced reticulation]
The assignments $w_u(p)=C_u(p)$ and $z_v(p)=R_v(p)$ define a reticulation on $\Grid$. For every $u,v,q,r$, there is a unique $p$ with $w_u(p)=q$ and $z_v(p)=r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each $u,v$, the cross-coordinate map is exactly $p\mapsto(C_u(p),R_v(p))$, which is bijective by cross-orthogonality.
\end{proof}

The same system can be serialized. Take $Y=\Grid$ and set $L(p,u)=C_u(p)$ and $T(p,v)=R_v(p)$.

\begin{theorem}[Svelte row encoding]
The row encoding of a cooperative system is a svelte semi-orthogonal array. For every $u,v,q,r$, exactly one row $y$ has $L(y,u)=q$ and $T(y,v)=r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rows are grid points, and the relevant projection is the orthogonality bijection for $(C_u,R_v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reticulation induced by a svelte array]
If $(Y,L,T)$ is a svelte semi-orthogonal array, then $w_u(y)=L(y,u)$ and $z_v(y)=T(y,v)$ define a reticulation on $Y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The defining array condition is exactly the required bijectivity of every cross-coordinate map. Fibres therefore form line families with unique cross-intersections.
\end{proof}

These constructions identify the shared coordinate content of incidence fibres, matrix systems, and balanced row arrays.

\section{Cardinality}
\begin{theorem}[Reticulation cardinality]
Let $P$ be a finite reticulation with parameters $(m,n)$. If $U$ and $V$ are nonempty, then
\[
|P|=mn.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $u\in U$ and $v\in V$. The map $p\mapsto(w_u(p),z_v(p))$ is a bijection from $P$ to $\I m\times\I n$. Therefore
\[
|P|=|\I m\times\I n|=|\I m|\,|\I n|=mn.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Svelte array row count]
If a svelte semi-orthogonal array has parameters $(m,n)$ and at least one coordinate column of each type, then its row set $Y$ satisfies $|Y|=mn$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Choose one left and one right column. Their projection is a bijection $Y\to\I m\times\I n$, or apply the preceding theorem to the induced reticulation.
\end{proof}

Additional coordinate families provide compatible descriptions of the same $mn$ points; they do not add points.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
To test whether $C$ is column-Latin, inspect each column and mark encountered symbols. Reject an out-of-range symbol or repetition. Since each column has $m$ entries from an $m$-element alphabet, absence of repetition implies completeness. This takes $O(mn)$ time and $O(m)$ reusable auxiliary space. The row-Latin test is dual, with $O(mn)$ time and $O(n)$ space.

To test orthogonality, scan all cells and mark $(C(i,j),R(i,j))$. Reject invalid or repeated pairs. There are $mn$ cells and $mn$ possible pairs, so distinctness implies bijectivity. Time and direct marker storage are $O(mn)$.

For $a=|U|$ column-type and $b=|V|$ row-type matrices, direct validation costs
\[
O\bigl(mn(a+b+ab)\bigr),
\]
covering $a+b$ Latin tests and $ab$ cross-orthogonality tests. Building a svelte array writes $a+b$ entries for each of $mn$ rows and therefore costs $O(mn(a+b))$ time and output space.

An inverse table indexed by $(q,r)$ can be built for a cooperative pair in $O(mn)$ time. It then answers each unique-intersection query in $O(1)$ time. The coordinate characterization theorems also permit both one-sided Latin tests to be implemented through a common orthogonality routine using $V$ or $H$.

\section{Example}
Let $m=3$, $n=4$, and set $C(i,j)=i$, $R(i,j)=j$. Each column of $C$ is $(0,1,2)$, each row of $R$ is $(0,1,2,3)$, and
\[
(C(i,j),R(i,j))=(i,j).
\]
Thus all twelve pairs occur once. A fixed value of $C$ selects a four-point horizontal fibre, while a fixed value of $R$ selects a three-point vertical fibre; they intersect once. The associated svelte array has the twelve rows
\[
(0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(0,3),
(1,0),(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),
(2,0),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3).
\]
Permuting entries separately within columns preserves column-Latinness, and permuting entries separately within rows preserves row-Latinness. Arbitrary simultaneous choices need not preserve orthogonality, showing that local balance and global cooperation are distinct.

\section{Applications and discussion}
In balanced experimental design, rows can represent units and the two coordinate types two classes of factors. Every selected cross-type factor pair then displays each ordered level pair exactly once, preventing cross-type confounding at index one.

For cross-view data indexing, each coordinate can be regarded as a categorical feature. Any one feature from each class uniquely identifies a record, while surjectivity certifies complete coverage. In benchmark construction, the same condition gives exact combinatorial balance across two groups of attributes. In communication or reversible transition systems, a left and a right label decode a unique state, while multiple coordinate pairs provide redundant lossless descriptions.

The asymmetry between $m$ and $n$ is structural. Column-Latin matrices use $m$ symbols because columns have $m$ positions; row-Latin matrices use $n$ symbols because rows have $n$ positions. Cross-pairs range over exactly $mn$ possibilities, matching the number of cells. No equality $m=n$ is needed.

The coordinate formulation treats lines as fibres rather than independently stored subsets, establishes direct conversions rather than a fully normalized structure-level equivalence, permits repeated coordinate families, and addresses exact index-one balance rather than higher multiplicities.

\section{Future directions}
Natural extensions include:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item introducing literal finite-set lines and pairwise-disjoint covers and proving equivalence with the fibre presentation;
\item proving full inverse correspondences among ordered reticulations, normalized cooperative systems, and svelte arrays;
\item modeling multiplicity and repetition-free families;
\item studying parastrophy and isotopy actions and their invariants;
\item developing prolongation, splicing, and direct-product constructions;
\item recovering nets, mixed orthogonal arrays, mutually orthogonal Latin squares, and reversible finite-state systems as special cases; and
\item enumerating small repetition-free systems up to isotopy with checkable counting certificates.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
A finite set with two types of coordinate families becomes rigid when every opposite-type pair labels it bijectively. Unique intersections force a rectangular grid with exactly $mn$ points. Column-Latin and row-Latin matrices describe the two directions, orthogonality supplies cooperation, and row-wise recording yields a svelte array. Fibres are lines, ordered symbol pairs are intersections, and cross-projection balance is the tabular form of the same bijection. This dictionary supports both structural generalization and direct linear-time validation of finite examples.

\end{document}

