# Explore the Mathematics of Cooperative Grids

A reticulation begins with two kinds of labels. When every left label and right label meet at exactly one point, the entire point set becomes a hidden rectangular grid. Start by changing the dimensions below.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The count is forced: with $m$ left labels and $n$ right labels, there are exactly $mn$ points. See the same fact as a labeled picture:

{{visualization:0}}

## From local Latin balance to global cooperation

A column-Latin matrix uses every one of its $m$ symbols once per column. A row-Latin matrix uses every one of its $n$ symbols once per row. Orthogonality adds the global demand that every ordered pair occurs once.

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the coordinate characterization</summary>

Pairing a matrix $C$ with the vertical coordinate $V(i,j)=j$ records a symbol and its column. This pairing is bijective exactly when every symbol occurs once in every column. Thus $C$ is column-Latin if and only if it is orthogonal to $V$. The row statement is dual.

{{algorithm:0}}
</details>

## Locate an intersection

Select a weft and warp label. Unique orthogonality identifies one cell.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the inverse-index algorithm</summary>

Scanning the $mn$ cells once builds a dictionary from each pair $(q,r)$ to its unique point. Construction takes $O(mn)$ time and space; subsequent intersection queries take expected $O(1)$ time.

{{algorithm:1}}
</details>

## Read geometry as a data table

Place one row in a table for every point and record all its left labels followed by all its right labels. Every projection onto one left and one right column reproduces the complete $m$-by-$n$ pair grid.

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the svelte-array construction</summary>

With $a$ left matrices and $b$ right matrices, each of the $mn$ cells contributes $a+b$ entries. The encoding therefore costs $O(mn(a+b))$ time and output space.

{{algorithm:2}}
</details>

## Break the rule deliberately

Orthogonality is stronger than local Latin balance. Replace one pair by a duplicate and watch unique decoding fail.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:0}}

For background, explore [Latin squares](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin_square), [orthogonal arrays](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orthogonal_array), and [combinatorial design theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combinatorial_design). These classical topics provide the broader landscape in which cooperative systems sit.

